 ## Background
-The intersection of rays with Gaussian primitives is central to the 3D Gaussian Splatting technique introduced by Kerbl et al. [G]. Standard rasterization-based 3DGS uses a first-order affine approximation to project 3D Gaussians to 2D screen space, which is efficient but fails for wide-field-of-view cameras or spherical projections due to perspective distortion and numerical instability in covariance inversion [S1]. Ray-based intersection methods were developed to address these limitations, offering exact geometric intersection for ellipsoidal splats [S1, S2]. Quadratic Gaussian Splatting extends this by using paraboloids to model complex surfaces, requiring intersection with second-order surfaces [S1]. The numerical stabilization techniques address the instability of solving quadratic equations for nearly degenerate splats [S1].
+The concept of ray-splat intersection evolved from the need to render 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) efficiently in real-time. Standard 3DGS uses a rasterization-based approach that approximates the projection of Gaussians onto the image plane. However, for wide-field-of-view cameras or spherical panoramas, this first-order affine approximation introduces significant artifacts [S1]. SPaGS addressed this by introducing an exact ray-splat intersection method using perpendicular planes to avoid covariance matrix inversion [S1]. Concurrently, methods like [Ray-Splat Intersection](6816-ray-splat-intersection.md) introduced BVH-based traversal for more complex scenes, supporting both spherical approximations and true ellipsoid intersections [S1, S2]. Numerical stability issues in quadratic solvers for quadric splats led to the development of stabilization techniques that switch to linear approximations when the quadratic coefficient is negligible [S1]. Quadratic Gaussian Splatting further generalized this by using paraboloids and geodesic distances for surface reconstruction [S1].
 
